> Validation and primary source

Native sanitizer tests enumerate 4096 six-position small count vectors and
compare their cost with exhaustive pair-merge dynamic programming, reconstruct
every actual minimum removal, and check lengths, Kraft sums and work. They
roundtrip 1365 binary/high-byte strings, check all 256 byte values, stable
input-order-independent byte choices, malformed
roots/children/cycles/maps/unreachable nodes, strict payload completion, wide
weights, complete quotas, ownership, summaries and native topology views. Frozen
comparisons preserve all 65536 ordinary count-length vectors and 1365 old byte
payload costs and old-book decodes. An independent arbitrary-precision Python
heap verifies 5123 count cases and 26799 actual merge events, including
full-width random counts and a 93-symbol Fibonacci case. All 35 original
consumer tests pass. Fresh documentation checks cover 92 definitions, 24
compiled public examples and four clangd hovers. All five complete MEP programs,
five inspected native figures and both computed-result exports pass, with
identical source/build/export images. The binary construction argument on page
1099 of the original paper was checked against the derivation here.

The original source is [David A. Huffman, A Method for the Construction of
Minimum-Redundancy
Codes](https://web.stanford.edu/class/ee398a/handouts/papers/Huffman%20-%20Min%20Redundancy%20Codes%20-%20IRE52.pdf),
Proceedings of the IRE 40(9), 1098–1101, 1952, [DOI
10.1109/JRPROC.1952.273898](https://doi.org/10.1109/JRPROC.1952.273898). This
report specializes to binary positive codewords and explicit integer weights,
with a forced singleton convention and a deterministic unsigned-byte tie rule.
It does not implement codebook serialization, packed output or a complete
archive format.
